 ### Latent Code Regularizer
-A specific regularization term $\mathcal{L}_l(z) = \|z\|_2$ applied to the latent codes in the autodecoder framework. It penalizes large magnitudes of $z$, keeping codes close to zero and enforcing similarity between different shapes in the learned space [S3]. A pitfall is that SDF and DDF (Directional Distance Function) models share the same latent code for a given shape, so the regularizer applies to both simultaneously [S3].
+A specific regularization term $\mathcal{L}_l(z) = \|z\|_2$ applied to the latent codes in the autodecoder framework [S3]. It penalizes large magnitudes of $z$ to keep latent codes close to zero, ensuring similarity between different shapes in the learned space [S3]. A pitfall is that SDF and DDF models must share the same latent code for a given shape to maintain consistency [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires joint optimization of network weights $\theta$ and latent vectors $z_j$. Inference for a new shape requires iterative optimization of $z$ (e.g., 800 iterations) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot L)$ to store $N$ latent vectors of dimension $L$. For $N=10$ shapes and $L=256$, this is negligible.
-*   **Performance:** On a single Nvidia V-100 GPU, training for 100k iterations takes significant time but produces high-quality reconstructions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables learning a continuous shape space; allows interpolation of shapes via latent vector averaging [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Inference is slow due to per-shape optimization; requires careful tuning of $\alpha$ and learning rates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires joint optimization of network weights and latent vectors. Test-time inference requires iterative optimization of $z$ (e.g., 800 iterations) per shape [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales linearly with the number of shapes $|J|$ due to the storage of $|J|$ latent vectors of size $L$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables representation of multiple shapes with a single network; allows interpolation of latent vectors to explore shape space [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Test-time inference is slow due to per-shape optimization; requires careful tuning of regularization parameter $\alpha$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When learning a shared shape space from multiple scans or when a single network must represent multiple instances without an explicit encoder [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the "Auto-Decoder" setup introduced by Park et al. (2019) in "Implicit Geometric Regularization for Learning Shapes" [S1, unofficial]. It was further popularized in works like SAL (Atzmon & Lipman, 2020) and applied to datasets like D-Faust for learning shape spaces of human meshes [S1, unofficial]. The concept of using latent codes to condition neural fields is also discussed in broader introductions to neural fields [S2, unofficial].
+The method originates from the auto-decoder setup introduced by Park et al. (2019) for learning shape spaces from raw scans [S1, unofficial]. It was applied to datasets like D-Faust for learning human shape and pose variations [S1, unofficial]. The approach is a variant of conditional neural fields where the condition is a learnable latent vector rather than a fixed input or generated weights [S2, unofficial].
 
